# Intuition
Since it was a matrix problem, we have to play with coordinates.

# Approach
https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/486c403a-f633-4675-82b9-c5345664f199
See when we move img2 up/down/R/L relative to img1 we'll be checking only the overlap part not the outer parts but the coordinates for img2 will change as row offset---> -n+1 to n-1  and col offset also will go from ----> -n+1 to n-1 for img2.

Now, they'll overlap only if img1[i][j] == img2[i+rowOffset][j+colOffset]  as the img2 will be shifted so coordinates adjusted accordingly. 

At last, don't forget to check boundary condition for i+rowOffset , j+colOffset as  it'll go out due to shifting of rows/columns. We have only to check for overlapping so ignore the outer going part i.e. out of boundary cells/data dropped.
 
class Solution {
    public int countMaxOverlap(int[][] a,int[][] b, int rowOffset,int colOffset){
        int cnt=0;
        for(int i =0;i<a.length;i++){
            for(int j = 0;j<a.length;j++){
                int i_new  = i+rowOffset;
                int j_new = j+colOffset;
                if(i_new < 0||i_new >= a.length || j_new < 0||j_new >= a.length)
                    continue;
                if(a[i][j] == 1 && b[i_new][j_new] == 1)
                    cnt++;
            }
        }
        return cnt;
    }
    public int largestOverlap(int[][] img1, int[][] img2) {
        int maxOverlap=0;
        int n = img1.length;
        for(int i = -n+1; i < n; i++){
            for(int j = -n+1; j< n;j++){
                maxOverlap = Math.max(maxOverlap,countMaxOverlap(img1,img2,i,j));
            }
        }
    return maxOverlap;
    }
}


